<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/business-document.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/business-document-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/business-document.webp 1200w" sizes="100vw" width="1200" height="900" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.1"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span aria-hidden="true">›</span>Glossary</nav><span class="kicker on-dark">Reference</span><h1>Searchable Insurance Claim Glossary</h1><p class="lede">40 terms from policies, estimates and appraisal awards, defined in plain English. Type in the box to filter.</p></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose"><div class="filter"><label for="gloss-filter">Search the glossary</label><input id="gloss-filter" type="search" placeholder="Try “ice dam” or “umpire”" autocomplete="off"></div><nav class="az" aria-label="Glossary letters"><a href="#g-A">A</a><a href="#g-C">C</a><a href="#g-D">D</a><a href="#g-E">E</a><a href="#g-F">F</a><a href="#g-H">H</a><a href="#g-I">I</a><a href="#g-L">L</a><a href="#g-M">M</a><a href="#g-N">N</a><a href="#g-O">O</a><a href="#g-P">P</a><a href="#g-R">R</a><a href="#g-S">S</a><a href="#g-U">U</a><a href="#g-V">V</a><a href="#g-X">X</a></nav><dl class="glossary"><dt id="g-A">Actual cash value (ACV)</dt><dd>The value of damaged property at the time of loss, usually replacement cost minus depreciation. Some policies pay only ACV.</dd><dt>Additional living expense (ALE)</dt><dd>Coverage for extra costs of living elsewhere while a home is repaired, often called Coverage D or loss of use.</dd><dt>Amount of loss</dt><dd>The dollar value of the damage. Appraisal is designed to decide this, not coverage.</dd><dt>Appraisal award</dt><dd>The written decision signed by two of the three panel members that sets the amount of loss.</dd><dt>Appraisal clause</dt><dd>The policy condition that lets either party demand appraisal when they disagree on the amount of loss.</dd><dt>Appraiser</dt><dd>A person each side names to value the loss. Policies usually require a competent and impartial appraiser.</dd><dt id="g-C">Collapse</dt><dd>An additional coverage in many policies. Some define it narrowly as an abrupt falling down.</dd><dt>Coverage A</dt><dd>Dwelling coverage for the house itself and attached structures.</dd><dt>Coverage B</dt><dd>Other structures coverage for detached garages, barns, sheds and fences.</dd><dt>Coverage C</dt><dd>Personal property, or contents, coverage.</dd><dt id="g-D">Declarations page</dt><dd>The summary page of a policy listing limits, deductibles, the insured property and endorsements.</dd><dt>Deductible</dt><dd>The part of a covered loss you pay before the policy pays.</dd><dt>Depreciation</dt><dd>A reduction in value for age, wear and obsolescence used to calculate ACV.</dd><dt id="g-E">Endorsement</dt><dd>A form that adds to, removes or changes coverage in the base policy.</dd><dt>Exclusion</dt><dd>Policy language removing certain causes or types of damage from coverage, such as flood.</dd><dt id="g-F">Flood</dt><dd>Generally, rising surface water from rivers, rain or melting snow. Usually excluded from homeowners policies and covered by separate flood insurance.</dd><dt id="g-H">Holdback</dt><dd>Recoverable depreciation withheld from the first payment until repairs are completed.</dd><dt id="g-I">Ice dam</dt><dd>A ridge of ice at the eaves that traps meltwater, which can back up under roofing and leak inside.</dd><dt>Impartial</dt><dd>Without bias toward either side. Many appraisal clauses require impartial or disinterested appraisers.</dd><dt id="g-L">Like kind and quality</dt><dd>Materials and workmanship equal to what existed before the loss.</dd><dt>Line item</dt><dd>One entry in an estimate, such as a quantity of shingles or a coat of paint, with a unit price.</dd><dt id="g-M">Matching</dt><dd>Whether new materials must match undamaged ones so the repair is uniform. Policy wording and state rules vary.</dd><dt>Mitigation</dt><dd>Reasonable steps after a loss to prevent further damage, such as tarping or water extraction.</dd><dt id="g-N">NFIP</dt><dd>The National Flood Insurance Program, which writes most residential flood policies through participating carriers.</dd><dt id="g-O">Ordinance or law</dt><dd>Coverage for added costs to meet building codes during repair. Often a percentage of Coverage A.</dd><dt>Overhead and profit (O&amp;P)</dt><dd>A general contractor’s charge for managing a job, often 10% overhead plus 10% profit.</dd><dt id="g-P">Peril</dt><dd>A cause of loss, such as fire, wind, hail or the weight of ice and snow.</dd><dt>Proof of loss</dt><dd>A sworn statement of the amount claimed, required by many policies when the insurer requests it.</dd><dt>Public adjuster</dt><dd>A licensed professional hired by the policyholder to prepare and negotiate the whole claim.</dd><dt id="g-R">Recoverable depreciation</dt><dd>Depreciation the insurer pays after repairs are completed under a replacement cost policy.</dd><dt>Replacement cost value (RCV)</dt><dd>The cost to repair or replace damaged property with like kind and quality, without deducting depreciation.</dd><dt id="g-S">Scope of loss</dt><dd>The list of damaged items and the repairs needed, the foundation of any estimate.</dd><dt>Snow load</dt><dd>The weight of snow and ice on a roof, measured in pounds per square foot.</dd><dt>Square</dt><dd>A roofing unit equal to 100 square feet of roof surface.</dd><dt>Sub-limit</dt><dd>A lower limit within a policy for a certain kind of property or loss, such as mold or trees.</dd><dt>Suit limitation</dt><dd>A policy condition setting how long you have to sue the insurer. Vermont law may affect how it applies.</dd><dt>Supplement</dt><dd>A request for additional payment for items missed or under-priced in the original estimate.</dd><dt id="g-U">Umpire</dt><dd>The neutral third panel member who decides the items the two appraisers cannot agree on.</dd><dt id="g-V">Vacancy</dt><dd>A condition in many policies that limits coverage when a building has been empty for a set period.</dd><dt id="g-X">Xactimate</dt><dd>Estimating software widely used by insurers and contractors, with regional price lists.</dd></dl><p class="gloss-none" hidden>No terms match that search.</p></article>
